 18. ADDITIONAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA (CONTINUED FROM SECTION 6)

The following criteria extend the list in Section 6. All criteria in both sections must be satisfied.

Security

51. Submitting a request to any /api/ endpoint with a JWT whose signature is invalid (tampered payload) returns 401 with {"detail": "Invalid or expired token"}.
52. Submitting a JWT whose exp claim is in the past returns 401.
53. The GET /api/health endpoint is reachable without authentication and returns 200 when the database is accessible.
54. The HTTP response to every endpoint (including static file serving) includes X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff and X-Frame-Options: DENY.
55. After 10 consecutive failed login attempts for the same email within 15 minutes, the 11th attempt returns 429 with a Retry-After header.
56. All SQL queries use parameterized statements — no string interpolation into SQL is present anywhere in main.py.
57. The raw value of any API token is returned only once in the POST /api/tokens response and never stored in plaintext in the database or in any log.

Streaming and Concurrency

58. Two concurrent SSE connections to the same transformation ID return an error on the second connection: {"type": "error", "message": "Already streaming"}.
59. If the EventSource connection drops midstream (network interruption), the transformation row remains in status = 'streaming' until a new SSE connection is opened. The new connection continues streaming from the beginning (the output is restreamed from the alreadyaccumulated full_output... actually: the second connection must detect that status = 'streaming' and return the "Already streaming" error as per acceptance criterion 58. After a configurable STALE_STREAMING_TIMEOUT_MINUTES environment variable (default 10), a background cleanup task (implemented as an asyncio periodic task started in the lifespan, running every 5 minutes) must reset any transformation stuck in status = 'streaming' for longer than the timeout to status = 'failed' with error_message = 'Streaming timed out.'. This allows the user to reopen the stream and retry.
60. The stale streaming cleanup task must log each row it resets: WARNING: Transformation {id} stuck in streaming state for >{timeout}m, marking failed.
61. When a completed transformation is reviewed via the SSE endpoint, the full output_text is delivered as a single {"type": "complete"} event followed by {"type": "done"} — not restreamed chunk by chunk.

Frontend Behavior

62. Pressing Ctrl+Enter on the New Transformation view with a valid input triggers the Generate button.
63. Pressing Escape while any confirmation modal is open closes it without performing the destructive action.
64. The character counter below the input textarea turns red (color var(--color-danger)) when the input exceeds the maximum character limit, and the Generate button becomes disabled.
65. The "Jump to bottom" pill button appears in the output panel only when the user has manually scrolled up during active streaming, and disappears when the stream ends or the user returns to the bottom.
66. All <label> elements in all forms have a for attribute that matches the id of the corresponding input. This applies to every form field across all six authenticated views.
67. On mobile viewports (≤ 640px), the navigation links collapse into a hamburger drawer. The hamburger button has aria-expanded set to true when the drawer is open and false when closed.
68. The password strength indicator on the registration and profile views updates in real time on every input event to the password field, showing a color bar and a text label ("Weak", "Fair", "Strong").
69. After logging out, pressing the browser Back button does not show authenticated content — the router detects that AppState.user is null and redirects to #/login.
70. When a pending redirect is stored in sessionStorage, logging in navigates to the stored route, consumes it from sessionStorage, and does not leave stale redirect data for subsequent logouts.

Admin

71. The admin statistics view shows transformations_last_24h as a count computed serverside using WHERE created_at >= datetime('now', '-24 hours').
72. Setting default_model via the admin config view changes the preselected model on the New Transformation page for all users on their next page load (the frontend must read default_model from the server on view render, not hardcode the default).
73. Setting min_input_length_chars to a value greater than or equal to max_input_length_chars via PUT /api/admin/config returns 422 with {"detail": "min_input_length_chars must be strictly less than max_input_length_chars"}.
74. The admin config form must fetch the current config values from GET /api/admin/config on every render (not cached) and prepopulate all fields except openai_api_key with the current values.

Deployment

75. Running docker-compose up --build in a directory containing the project files results in a running application accessible at http://localhost:8000 within 30 seconds, assuming a valid .env file with JWT_SECRET is present.
76. The GET /api/health Docker HEALTHCHECK passes within 15 seconds of container startup.
77. Stopping and restarting the container preserves all user accounts, transformations, and configuration stored in the Docker volume.
78. The application runs as a nonroot user inside the Docker container (USER appuser in Dockerfile).

Data Integrity

79. Deleting a user account (if that endpoint were to be implemented in the future) would cascadedelete all their refresh tokens, API tokens, transformations, and rate limit log entries — the foreign key ON DELETE CASCADE constraints enforce this at the database level.
80. All datetime values inserted into the database are in UTC and formatted as ISO8601 strings ending in no timezone offset (i.e., pure UTC without a Z suffix or +00:00, using Python's datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')).

