 33. ACCESSIBILITY REQUIREMENTS (WCAG 2.1 AA)

The application must meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA. The following specific requirements are mandatory:

Color contrast: All body text against its background must have a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1. All large text (≥ 18pt or ≥ 14pt bold) must have a ratio of at least 3:1. With the defined CSS custom properties: --color-text-primary (e2e8f0) on --color-bg-primary (0f1117) achieves approximately 13.8:1 — compliant. --color-text-secondary (8892b0) on --color-bg-primary achieves approximately 5.1:1 — compliant. --color-text-muted (4a5568) on --color-bg-primary achieves approximately 2.6:1 — this is used only for placeholder text, which is exempt from the 4.5:1 requirement under WCAG 1.4.3.

Focus indicators: Every focusable element must have a visible focus indicator. All buttons and inputs have focus-visible outlines defined in the button CSS system above. Add the following global CSS rule:

css
:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--color-border-focus);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* Remove default focus ring only when focus-visible is supported */
:focus:not(:focus-visible) {
  outline: none;
}


Screen reader announcements: The output panel's <pre id="output-pre"> element must have aria-live="polite" and aria-label="Generated implementation prompt". However, because updating a live region on every streaming chunk would flood screen readers, add aria-live="polite" but update aria-label to reflect completion status only (not chunk by chunk). After streaming completes, announce: pre.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Generated implementation prompt — complete. Use the action buttons to copy or download.').

Skip navigation: Add a skip link as the very first element inside <body>:

html
<a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to main content</a>


With CSS:

css
.skip-link {
  position: absolute;
  top: -100px;
  left: 0;
  background: var(--color-accent-primary);
  color: white;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  z-index: 10000;
  border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-md) 0;
  transition: top var(--transition-fast);
}
.skip-link:focus {
  top: 0;
}


Add id="main-content" to the <div id="app-root"> element.

Form error association: Every field error <span> must have a unique id (e.g., email-error, password-error). Each corresponding input must reference it via aria-describedby. When Validator.showFieldError sets an error, it must also call input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true'). When Validator.clearFieldError is called, it must call input.removeAttribute('aria-invalid').



 34. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS

The following performance constraints are mandatory and must be addressed within the singlefile architecture:

Backend response times: For all nonstreaming endpoints, the P95 response time must be under 200ms on hardware equivalent to a singlecore 2GHz CPU with the SQLite database on a local disk. Achieve this by:
 Using SQLite WAL mode (enabled in DDL).
 Keeping all connections shortlived (open, execute, close).
 Not performing blocking I/O inside async route handlers (all DB operations are synchronous but fast enough for SQLite; longer operations must be run in asyncio.get_event_loop().run_in_executor(None, sync_function) if they exceed 50ms).

Frontend bundle size: The compiled index.html file must not exceed 500KB total (including all inline CSS, JavaScript, and HTML). Tailwind CSS is loaded from CDN (not bundled) and does not count toward this limit.

Frontend render time: Each view must perform its initial DOM render (before data fetches complete) in under 16ms (one frame at 60fps). This means view functions must synchronously construct the skeleton/loading state first, then initiate async data fetches, then replace skeletons with real content. No view function is permitted to await an API call before rendering anything to the DOM.

Pagination and list performance: The history view must never fetch more than page_size rows in a single request. The total row count is fetched separately via the same query with a COUNT wrapper. Never fetch all rows to count them in JavaScript.

SSE buffering: The StreamingResponse must not buffer the entire response before sending. Each yield in the async generator must result in an immediate flush to the client. Confirm by setting proxy_buffering off in the nginx config and verifying with a browser that tokens appear within 500ms of the model generating them.



 35. DATA EXPORT ENDPOINT

Add the following endpoint to main.py:

GET /api/users/me/export

This endpoint allows authenticated users to download all their data in JSON format (GDPR data portability). Requires get_authenticated_user.

Processing:
1. Fetch the user row (excluding password_hash).
2. Fetch all of the user's transformations (all columns including full output_text).
3. Fetch all of the user's API tokens (excluding token_hash).
4. Construct the response object:
json
{
  "exported_at": "<current_utc_iso8601>",
  "user": {
    "id": <int>,
    "email": "<string>",
    "display_name": "<string>",
    "role": "<string>",
    "created_at": "<iso>",
    "last_login_at": "<iso|null>"
  },
  "transformations": [ ... ],
  "api_tokens": [ ... ]
}

5. Return as JSONResponse with header Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="my-data-{user_id}-{YYYYMMDD}.json" and Content-Type: application/json.

Add a corresponding "Export My Data" button to the Profile view (#/profile). Place it in its own section below the profile edit form, styled with btn-secondary. Clicking it calls GET /api/users/me/export via fetch directly (not apiCall, because we need the raw response for download), then creates a Blob from the response body and triggers a download via a temporary <a> element.



